# Fableland — Wonder Items GDD

Design doc for the wonder-item layer: slots, acquisition, anatomy (passive / skill /
cooldown), features/tags, lifecycle patterns, and the item catalog.

Implementation target: `Scripts/Items/`. The next runtime landing is the **v0.10.0
combat-item prototype**; the earlier v0.6.0 landing supplied only catalog display data,
one held slot, and Transportation-Hub equipment management.

> **Status (v0.12.8):** all fourteen catalog items are live, with a source each (§1.2). The
> item layer's run state is one ledger (`Scripts/Run/ItemLedger.cs`, saved as the optional
> `RunSaveData.ItemLedger`) written only by `RunState`'s item verbs (`Scripts/Run/RunState.Items.cs`):
> perishing (§4.1), Possession and its enchant (§4.4), the plantation (§4.3), THE VOID's phantoms,
> the Weird Mushroom's morning after, Pixolotl's Feather's rewind and the QiongFeng Claw. The day
> end runs them in `PerishStep` (`RunState.RunItemDay`). Combat halves are `ItemRuntime`
> behaviours: DD (a creature, `Scripts/Items/DdCreature.cs`), the mushroom, the Feather/Wish
> and Blessing passives; a Possession item's passive is put on the body in play from wherever
> it lies (passive-only runtimes). Since **v0.12.10** the hub's room has a **plantation** and an
> **enchanting altar** station (`PlantationPanel`, `EnchantPanel`), and since **v0.12.12** THE
> VOID's skill darkens a fight (`VoidBlindness`, on the Black Prison's sight cap). **Still open:**
> the phantom's BOSS fight (a −25 % HP touch stands in), rolled inheritance (60–140 %) on
> harvests, and the mushroom's outcome (f). Each is marked below.
>
> *(v0.10.0: every held item is an instance with persistent day/second cooldowns; FanChen's
> Heart, Yukai's Rope, The Forgotten Kashaya, Pome's Bravery and Pome's Seed live; TwistedReality
> live as a possession rewind and Eidolon-bridge map skill.)*

---

## 1. Overview

Wonder items are **equipment** that modify stats, grant activatable skills, or provide
passive effects. They are the primary horizontal-progression layer within a run — more
items mean more options, but only a few can be active at once.

### 1.1 Slots

| Container | Capacity | Effect |
|-----------|----------|--------|
| **Held slot** | 1 per protagonist (3 max with a full party) | Passive is active; skill is usable; its applicable cooldown ticks (days at day-end; seconds in combat). |
| **Backpack** | Unlimited | Items are carried but **inert** — no passive, skill unavailable, and ordinary cooldowns do **not** tick. |

The player can swap items between backpack and held slots at any **Transportation Hub**
(which currently reuses the shelter service scene; see `NODES.gdd` §5.1). Held slots are
per-protagonist.

**Backpack presentation (v0.10.1):** selecting a held-item bubble in Team Build opens a
five-column grid of square icons. It contains every backpack instance plus all currently held
instances; a held icon is grey and tilted but can still be clicked to swap it with the selected
holder. The backpack portion is ordered by the catalog's stable display order, with live
prototype items first; hovering an icon opens a cursor-adjacent label containing its name,
passive, and skill. The approved master sheet and independently replaceable icon files live in
`Assets/Sprites/Items/WonderItems/`.

**Benching rule:** when a protagonist is benched, their held item **auto-returns to
the backpack**. Team build and item assignment can only be changed at a shelter node,
so the player cannot bench a protagonist mid-combat or mid-exploration to shuffle items.

> The party cap and held-slot cap can be modified by certain items or mechanisms
> (e.g., "A Weird Mushroom" outcome f).

### 1.2 Acquisition

| Source | Likelihood |
|--------|-----------|
| Combat node — mission reward | Guaranteed for first-successful completion of certain nodes |
| Foe drop | Very low chance on kill (see `FOES.gdd` §7 loot hooks; **TODO:** add wonder-item row to the FOES loot table) |
| Question-mark node — story event | Variable, depending on the event |
| VOID trader | Barter with wonder cores or other items (see `NODES.gdd` §5.3) |
| Jousting | The wanderer's held item transfers on victory |
| Plantation harvest | Harvesting a planted item yields its fruit |
| Item conversion | Some items transform into others after use |
| **TODO** | Foes may rarely **hold** a wonder item and use it in combat; killing them drops it |

**Current prototype rewards:** Protect grants FanChen's Heart, Destroy grants The Forgotten
Kashaya, an outer BOSS grants Yukai's Rope, and the Abandoned Cart event grants Pome's Bravery.
The first starting-realm LV4 victory additionally grants TwistedReality.

**v0.12.8 sources** (every item now has one; events are append-only in `EventDefs`):

| Item | Source |
|------|--------|
| DD | "A Peddler of Small Wonders" — 14 wonder cores |
| The Blessing of Shieldon | the Peddler — 9 cores |
| A Weird Mushroom | the Peddler — 3 cores; "A Ring of Mushrooms" — free; a Rotten Mushroom's harvest (4) |
| A Rotten Mushroom | a Weird Mushroom left uneaten for 2 days |
| Pixolotl's Feather | "A Pool Under the Moon" — −15 % HP; Pixolotl's Wish's harvest (2) |
| Pixolotl's Wish | a Feather's rewind |
| THE VOID | "A Black Door" — opened, with 25 cores (the only event whose gift is a curse) |
| QiongFeng Emperor's Claw | "A Claw in the Riverbed" — −15 % HP; the **Palace of LOOING**'s capital, on its first fall (it is the claw's own realm) |
| Pome's Bravery | the Abandoned Cart event; Pome's Seed's harvest (2) |

---

## 2. Item anatomy

Every wonder item has up to four components:

| Component | Required? | Description |
|-----------|-----------|-------------|
| **Passive** | Optional | Always-on effect while the item is **held** (or always, if it has the `Possession` tag). |
| **Skill** | Optional | An activatable ability. May be combat-only, map-only, or either. |
| **Cooldown** | Only if the item has a Skill | How long before the skill can be used again. Two types: day-based and second-based (§3). |
| **Features / Tags** | Optional | Special behaviours: perishable, convertible, plantable, possession, eternal (§4). |

An item with no passive, no skill, and no features is a **dormant** item (e.g., a
consumed remnant waiting to be planted). Dormant items still occupy their slot.

---

## 3. Cooldown system

There are **two cooldown axes**, independent of each other:

### 3.1 Day-based cooldown (world-map / exploration skills)

Measured in **days**. At **day-end resolution** (when the player clicks "Finish the Day";
see `NODES.gdd` §7), every **held** item's cooldown decrements by 1. Items in the
backpack do **not** tick down.

| CD | Behaviour |
|----|-----------|
| 0 (no CD) | Passive only; no skill. |
| 1 day | Can be used once per day. Resets at day-end resolution. |
| 2 days | Use → 2 full day-end resolutions must pass → usable again on day 3. |
| Single-use | Use once → skill is consumed. The item may convert or become dormant. |

Day-based skills can be used **either** during combat **or** on the world map,
depending on the item's design. Some items specify "combat only" or "map only."

### 3.2 Second-based cooldown (combat skills)

Measured in **real-time seconds** during combat. Functions identically to protagonist
ability cooldowns — ticks down with the arena clock, unaffected by the day cycle.

| Example CD | Item |
|------------|------|
| 15 s | The Forgotten Kashaya (knockback) |
| 30 s | FanChen's Heart (self-Freeze), Yukai's Rope (grapple) |

An item may have **both** a day-based CD and a second-based CD if it has two skills
(not currently spec'd, but the architecture supports it).

### 3.3 Silenced: no item (v0.12.13, the owner)

A **silenced** fighter (a Black Prison holds them — `PumpKing.gdd` §Q) **uses no item**: F is
refused with a toast ("Silenced: … can't be used until it lifts") and **nothing is spent** —
no cooldown starts, no day is counted, a single-use item is not used up. The item's
**passive keeps working** (a silence stops what you do, not what you carry), and so does a
Possession item's. Checked first, before either cooldown (`ItemRuntime.TryUse`). Today only a
BOSS PumpKing's prison could silence the player, and his brain does not cast it yet (TBD #79).

---

## 4. Feature / tag catalog

Tags modify how the item behaves in the world. An item can carry **multiple** tags.

### 4.1 `Perishable`

If not used within N days, the item is destroyed (or converts to a rotten/expired
form). The countdown ticks at **day-end resolution regardless of whether the
item is held or in the backpack**. A perishable item in the backpack will still rot.

| Perish timer | Consequence on expiry |
|-------------|----------------------|
| N days | Item is removed from inventory; may be replaced by a "rotten" variant. |

*Example: A Weird Mushroom — 2 days → A Rotten Mushroom.*

### 4.2 `Convertible`

After the item's skill is used (or a condition is met), the item is **replaced** by
a different item in the same slot. The new item inherits the slot position (held or
backpack). Convertible items cannot also be `Eternal` — the two tags are mutually
exclusive (§4.5).

*Examples:*
- Pome's Bravery → Pome's Seed (after skill use)
- Pixolotl's Feather → Pixolotl's Wish (after skill use)
- A Weird Mushroom → A Rotten Mushroom (perish expiry)

### 4.3 `Plantable`

The item can be **planted** in a plantation slot at a shelter node (see `NODES.gdd`
§5.2 and the separate **Plantation GDD**). Plantable items have:

| Attribute | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Harvest time** | Days until the plant bears fruit. |
| **Fruit** | A list of wonder items produced at harvest (quantity and type). |
| **Inheritance** | Optional — the fruit's stats may inherit a range (e.g., 60%–140%) of the parent item's attributes, rolled at harvest time. |

*Examples:*
- Pome's Seed — 7 days; the harvest output is **TBD**, while every inherited stat rolls
  60%–140% of the father's rolled stats
- Pixolotl's Wish — 7 days, harvests 2× Pixolotl's Feather
- A Rotten Mushroom — 4 days, generates 4× Weird Mushrooms with inherited HP range + random consequence

### 4.4 `Possession`

The item's **passive is always active**, even when the item is in the backpack (not
held). The skill still requires the item to be held.

**Scope:** a `Possession` passive applies to **every protagonist in the active team
build** (party-scope). A held item's normal passive applies to its holder only; if a
held item also carries `Possession`, the passive is applied once party-wide, never
twice to the holder.

*Example: THE VOID — its phantom-spawning passive works from the backpack.*

The `Possession` tag can also be **added** to another item via certain meta-items
(e.g., The Blessing of Shieldon's skill). However, `Eternal` items resist this —
they cannot be enchanted (§4.5).

### 4.5 `Eternal`

The item **cannot** be:
- Traded (VOID trader rejects it)
- Enchanted / upgraded (resists modification by meta-items such as Blessing of Shieldon)
- Deserted / dropped / voluntarily removed from inventory
- **Converted** into another item (mutually exclusive with the `Convertible` tag)

This is typically a **negative** tag attached to cursed or burdensome items.
`Eternal` + `Possession` together mean the item is a permanent, inescapable modifier
for the entire run.

*Example: THE VOID — Eternal + Possession; the phantoms follow you forever.*

---

## 5. Item lifecycle patterns

Items fall into several lifecycle archetypes:

### 5.1 Static
Held → provides passive ± skill on cooldown → stays the same item forever.
*Examples: FanChen's Heart, Yukai's Rope, The Forgotten Kashaya, DD.*

### 5.2 Single-use → convert
Held → skill used → converts to a successor item. The successor may be dormant,
plantable, or a different static item.
*Examples: Pome's Bravery → Pome's Seed, Pixolotl's Feather → Pixolotl's Wish,
The Blessing of Shieldon (consumed on use).*

### 5.3 Perishable → convert
Held or in backpack → timer expires → converts to rotten/dormant form.
*Example: A Weird Mushroom → A Rotten Mushroom.*

### 5.4 Plantable → harvest
Planted at shelter → grows over N days → harvested for fruit items. The planted
item is consumed (removed from inventory) when planted.
*Examples: Pome's Seed, Pixolotl's Wish, A Rotten Mushroom.*

### 5.5 Conditional trigger
Held → condition met (e.g., boss killed) → effect fires → item may be consumed.
*Example: QiongFeng Emperor of LOONG's Claw — boss kill triggers loot sweep +
world crack + teleport, then claw faints.*

---

## 6. Classification

### 6.1 By usage domain

| Domain | Description | Examples |
|--------|-------------|----------|
| **Combat** | Passive + skill both function inside the arena. Cooldown is normally second-based, unless the item definition explicitly uses a day CD. | FanChen's Heart, Yukai's Rope, The Forgotten Kashaya, THE VOID (skill is combat-only) |
| **Exploration** | Passive and/or skill affect the overworld map. Cooldown is normally day-based. | QiongFeng Emperor's Claw, TwistedReality |
| **Hybrid** | Combat passive + world-map skill, or vice versa. May have both CD types. | DD (combat passive + day-based skill), Pome's Bravery (combat passive + single-use skill → plantable), Pixolotl's Feather (combat passive + day-rewind skill), A Weird Mushroom (single-use combat buff + next-day world effect) |

### 6.2 By lifecycle archetype

See §5 — Static, Single-use→convert, Perishable→convert, Plantable→harvest, Conditional trigger.

### 6.3 By tag matrix

| Item | Perishable | Convertible | Plantable | Possession | Eternal |
|------|-----------|-------------|-----------|------------|---------|
| Blessing of Shieldon | — | — | — | — (grants it) | — |
| THE VOID | — | — | — | ✓ | ✓ |
| DD | — | — | — | — | — |
| Pome's Bravery | — | ✓ (→Seed) | — | — | — |
| Pome's Seed | — | — | ✓ (7d) | — | — |
| FanChen's Heart | — | — | — | — | — |
| Yukai's Rope | — | — | — | — | — |
| Pixolotl's Feather | — | ✓ (→Wish) | — | — | — |
| Pixolotl's Wish | — | — | ✓ (7d) | — | — |
| Forgotten Kashaya | — | — | — | — | — |
| QiongFeng Emperor's Claw | — | — (consumed on trigger) | — | — | — |
| TwistedReality | — | — | — | — | — |
| A Weird Mushroom | ✓ (2d) | ✓ (→Rotten) | — | — | — |
| A Rotten Mushroom | — | — | ✓ (4d) | — | — |

> **Rule:** `Eternal` and `Convertible` are mutually exclusive. No item in the catalog
> carries both, and no future item should.

### 6.4 Shared status-duration modifiers

Some items extend an existing status: Pome's Bravery / Seed extend **OnFire** by
0.2 s, and FanChen's Heart extends **Frozen** by 0.2 s. The extension applies to
each status application received by the holder, not once to the aggregate stack.

> **Implementation note (v0.10.0):** the combat-status layer uses source-aware queued duration
> extensions, so these modifiers delay stack decay without becoming extra stacks or damage.

---

## 7. Item catalog (reference)

### 7.1 The Blessing of Shieldon

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Exploration (meta-item) |
| Lifecycle | Single-use → consumed |
| Passive | +10% SPD |
| Skill | **Single use.** Enchant the `Possession` tag onto a target wonder item in your inventory. The Blessing is then consumed. Cannot target `Eternal` items. |
| CD | — (single use) |
| Tags | — |

> **Built (v0.12.8):** the passive is ×1.1 move speed on the holder (`ShieldonBehavior`). The
> enchant is `RunState.TryEnchantPossession(target)`: the Blessing must be held by a team
> member; the target may be any other item that is not `Eternal` and not already Possession
> (so not TwistedReality or THE VOID); the enchantment belongs to the item *instance*
> (`ItemLedger.EnchantedPossession`) and survives its conversions. **The altar (v0.12.10):** a hub's
> enchanting altar is open while a team member holds the Blessing; its panel lists every owned
> item (Eternal and already-Possession ones say why not), asks once, and enchants. In a fight
> **F** only says where it is spent. TODO(art): the altar's glyph, an enchanted item's mark.

### 7.2 THE VOID

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Hybrid (exploration passive, combat skill) |
| Lifecycle | Static (permanent burden) |
| Passive | **`Eternal` `Possession`.** Every 4 days, a **VOID PHANTOM** spawns on the overworld map. The player's vision is shared by all phantoms — they see what you see. Each time the player challenges a new node, all phantoms move one step toward the player's position. |
| Skill | **Combat only.** All foes lose their vision for 10 seconds. |
| CD | 2 days (day-based) |
| Tags | `Possession`, `Eternal` |

> **Design note:** THE VOID is a cursed item — once acquired, it cannot be removed.
> The phantoms create escalating map pressure: the more you explore, the closer they get.
> When a phantom reaches the player's position, it triggers a **BOSS fight** at that node.
> (Phantom contact mechanics are deferred to a future content pass — the spawning and
> movement rules are kept as placeholders.)

> **Built (v0.12.8):** while THE VOID is owned anywhere (Possession), a clock counts day ends;
> every **4** a phantom rises on a node chosen from the run's own stream among nodes the VOID
> has not eaten at least **3 roads** from you. **Challenging** a combat node you have not
> conquered (a retry counts) moves every phantom one road along a shortest path toward it, at
> node entry — before the battle team's HP is taken — so a touch is felt in that fight.
> Devoured ground still carries a phantom (it is the VOID's own). **Placeholder for the BOSS
> fight:** a phantom that reaches you touches the party for **25 % of every fighter's HP**
> (`HurtParty`, never below 5 %) and is gone; the arena says so as it opens. Phantoms are drawn
> on the map (a hooded shape with pale eyes — **TODO(art)**), and the map's info line counts
> them and the days to the next.
>
> **The skill (v0.12.12):** held, **F** in a fight starts its 2 days and darkens the fight for
> **10 s** (`VoidBlindness`): every hostile target — foes, objectives, a boss — gets a sight cap
> of 0 under the cast's own source (the reaction the Black Prison brought: a capped foe drops
> the player at once and a blow does not turn it; a boss brain finds no one). A foe that arrives
> during the darkness is blinded as it comes. The darkness lives in the arena, so a Tab switch
> does not lift it; when it ends, or the arena goes, every cap it laid is cleared. TODO(art): the
> darkness itself.

### 7.3 DD

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Hybrid |
| Lifecycle | Static |
| Passive | Each time you pick up a wonder core, heal 2% of max HP. |
| Skill | **Combat.** Release DD — a small bug-like creature that **crouches** on the ground. DD moves along the ground, devouring everything damageable in its path for **200 damage**. It turns around on hitting a wall. The player must **reclaim** DD (walk into it) to stow it again. |
| CD | 2 days (day-based) |
| Tags | — |

> **Design note:** The game does not pause when a combat node's goal is achieved or
> the timer expires — DD can still be reclaimed during the post-combat window. If the
> player leaves the node without reclaiming DD, it is **set free** (lost permanently).
> The item is removed from inventory. Don't forget to claim it back.

> **Built (v0.12.8):** **F** lets DD go at the holder's feet, facing the holder's way, and
> starts its 2 days. DD (`DdCreature`, built in code) crawls along Ground and Platforms at
> **4 m/s**, devours every hostile target it touches for **200** — **each once per release**
> (an invincible one is skipped and can still be devoured later) — and turns at walls. It
> collides with nothing but terrain; if it falls for 3 s (off the map's edge) it is put back
> where it was let go. The body the player wears reclaims it by walking into it (within 1 m),
> not before 0.6 s have passed; any party member's body can. **Finish the Day**, or quitting
> after the goal resolved, with DD still loose sets it free (`RunState.SetItemFree`; the day
> summary says so). The passive heals 2 % max HP per wonder core picked up in a fight.
> **TODO(art):** DD's sprite and crawl/devour animations (a drawn shell stands in).

### 7.4 Pome's Bravery

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Hybrid |
| Lifecycle | Single-use → Convertible |
| Passive | OnFire duration +0.2 s. While OnFire: **+5% omni-vamp** (lifesteal on all damage dealt). |
| Skill | **Single use.** Set oneself on fire (60 OnFire stacks). After use, converts to **Pome's Seed**. |
| CD | — (single use) |
| Tags | `Convertible` |

### 7.5 Pome's Seed

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| Domain | Exploration (plantable) |
| Lifecycle | Plantable → harvest |
| Passive | OnFire duration +0.2 s. While OnFire: +5% omni-vamp. |
| Skill | — (dormant skill slot) |
| CD | — |
| Tags | `Plantable` |
| Harvest time | **7 days** |
| Fruit | **TBD.** Every inheritable stat on the harvest output rolls **60%–140%** of the father's corresponding rolled stat. |

> **Implementation note (v0.10.0):** Bravery and Seed now share their OnFire duration and
> 5%-damage-heal passive in combat. Bravery's **F** skill grants 60 OnFire stacks and converts
> the held instance into Seed immediately. The plantation system does not yet exist, so Seed's
> planting/7-day harvest remains unavailable rather than being simulated through the backpack.
>
> **Decision (v0.12.8):** the fruit the GDD left TBD is **2× Pome's Bravery** — its parent,
> twice, like the Wish's two Feathers. The plantation is live (§4.3 note); rolled inheritance
> (60–140 % of the father's stats) waits for per-instance rolled stats, so a fruit today is a
> plain Bravery.

